WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.530
LINKEDIN:
What’s the most powerful social media in

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:03.530
the world?

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:04.530
YouTube?

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.530
Instagram?

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:06.530
TikTok?

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:12.000
Well, there’s definitely a strong argument
to be made about each and every one of those

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.340
platforms, but I would argue that the most
powerful social media in the world is actually

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.710
LinkedIn.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:29.119
If you’re not familiar with LinkedIn, they’re
a professional networking site owned by Microsoft

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:32.880
where people can share their career progression
with friends, family, and hopefully also recruiters

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.840
from potential future employers.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:41.260
Today, LinkedIn boasts a whopping 950 million
members from more than 200 countries and territories

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.559
around the world.

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:48.630
For perspective, WhatsApp has over 2 billion
monthly active users and YouTube has 2.7 billion

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:49.890
monthly active users.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:55.980
So, LinkedIn is obviously not the most popular
social media platform in the world but when

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.270
we look at power, it’s a completely different
story.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:01.730
Take revenue for example.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.550
In 2022, WhatsApp pulled in a total of $906
million.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:10.790
This translates to a little less to 50 cents
per user per year.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:16.390
LinkedIn on the other hand pulled in $14.59
billion in 2022.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:22.610
This translates to over $15 in revenue per
user per year or over 30 times more than WhatsApp.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.890
Aka, at their current rate, WhatsApp would
need over 60 billion users to pull in the

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.200
same revenue as LinkedIn.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.280
Even YouTube only pulls in a little less than
$11 per user per year and a lot of people

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.990
spend their entire lives on YouTube.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.399
With LinkedIn, the vast majority of users
just check in here and there.

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:46.640
And the craziest part is that Microsoft didn’t
pay all that much more for LinkedIn than Facebook

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.079
paid for WhatsApp.

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:53.070
When all was said and done, Facebook paid
a total of $22 billion for WhatsApp while

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.689
Microsoft paid $26.2 billion for LinkedIn.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:02:01.610
Microsoft clearly got more bang for their
buck but the power of LinkedIn goes much deeper

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.150
than just monetary gain.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:09.780
Today, LinkedIn is synonymous with not just
professional networking but power, control,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.500
and influence within the corporate world.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.360
Let me explain.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:23.099
SUPERIOR DEMOGRAPHICS:
Microsoft has clearly built a massive social

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:28.959
media platform behind the scenes, but that’s
not the only they’ve been building.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:34.319
Over the past 15 years, Microsoft has grown
their cash reserves from $30 to $40 billion

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:37.290
to $130 to $140 billion.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:42.599
If you’re wondering how such companies responsibly
store and maintain so much cash, well, their

