00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:41.659
But, anyway, taking a look back, the story
of LinkedIn dates back to the early 2000s

00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:44.549
to a man named Reid Hoffman.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:48.599
LinkedIn likes to claim LinkedIn was started
in Reid’s living room and that’s probably

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:55.079
technically true but make no mistake, LinkedIn
was by no means a bootstrapped startup.

00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:59.939
Not only did they have a stacked team with
a bunch of early PayPal employees and executives

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:05.700
but they also had access to $123 million worth
of funding over the next several years.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.849
So, you basically had the dream team paired
with virtually unlimited funding, and their

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:15.030
goal was simple: create a business oriented
social media network.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:21.320
They would call it LinkedIn and LinkedIn would
make its debut on May 5, 2003.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:26.290
Unlike other social media platforms, LinkedIn
never really had some sort of viral moment

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:27.410
or explosion.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:33.060
Instagram, for example, racked up 25,000 users
within first day, 100,000 users within the

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.979
first week, and a 1,000,000 users within the
first 2 months.

00:04:36.979 --> 00:04:40.500
LinkedIn’s growth on the other hand was
a lot more modest.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.950
They ended the first month with 4,500 users
much of whom were invited by the company’s

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.050
original employees.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.690
But, overtime, these numbers slowly compounded.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:53.210
Instead of getting thousands of users per
month, LinkedIn started getting tens of thousands

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.789
and then hundreds of thousands and so on.

00:04:55.789 --> 00:05:01.210
Likely the main reason that LinkedIn never
had a viral moment was because of their demographics

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:06.050
but at the same time, their unique demographics
are what make the platform so special in the

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.190
first place.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:12.280
Technically, you only have to be 16 years
old to create a LinkedIn account but I don’t

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:16.120
think you’d be surprised to hear that the
vast majority of LinkedIn users are above

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.020
the age of 25.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:22.479
This is stark contrast to basically any other
social media platform.

00:05:22.479 --> 00:05:28.340
With TikTok, for example, 30% of their users
are under the age of 20 and another 30% are

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.680
under the age of 30.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:35.370
YouTube’s users tend to be older but we
also have to keep in mind: what kid doesn’t

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:36.889
just lie about their age?

00:05:36.889 --> 00:05:42.750
After all, 5 out of the top 10 most subscribed
YouTubers make content for teenagers and children.

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.229
There’s nothing wrong with this as YouTube
and most social media platforms are largely

00:05:47.229 --> 00:05:48.430
meant for entertainment.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:53.509
So, it’s not surprising that younger people
with more time tend to use these platforms

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:54.509
more actively.

00:05:54.509 --> 00:05:59.930
But, when we look at from a more cynical perspective
of which userbase is more lucrative and useful

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:04.030
to control, it’s obviously the older demographic.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:08.910
We should also note that LinkedIn’s userbase
is not only older than other platforms, they’re

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.950
also way more successful, at least from career
perspective.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:17.139
I mean, just think about it, what type of
person would want to publicize their professional

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:18.139
life.

00:06:18.139 --> 00:06:22.460
Obviously, not someone who’s a cashier at
Walmart or an Uber Eats delivery driver.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:27.320
It’s probably not even someone who has a
more modest job as a teacher or police officer

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.449
or nurse or even doctor for that matter.

00:06:30.449 --> 00:06:34.740
LinkedIn’s most active users are the hyper
competitive type who are playing the game

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.539
of capitalism and climbing the corporate ladder.

00:06:37.539 --> 00:06:41.669
Aka, the people with the most power, control,
and influence in the world.

00:06:41.669 --> 00:06:48.500
By the end of 2004, LinkedIn would grow to
house over a million of these people and that

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:52.120
was just the beginning.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:58.960
CRYSTAL CLEAR INTENT:
Moving into 2005, LinkedIn would roll out

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:04.830
the first stages of monetizing this highly
valuable user base with the launch of LinkedIn

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:05.830
jobs.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:11.410
Over the years, this has expanded into 3 primary
sources of revenue starting with talent solutions.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:14.199
This is by far their largest source of revenue.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:18.270
Basically, companies and recruitment agencies
pay LinkedIn to be able to post their jobs

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:22.940
listings on the website and easily reach out
to highly qualified potential recruits.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:27.460
After Microsoft tookover, we haven’t been
getting as detailed reports about LinkedIn’s

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:32.320
revenue segments, but before they tookover,
talent solutions accounted for almost 2/3s

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.680
of LinkedIn’s revenue.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.130
Aside from talent solutions, just like any
other social media platform, LinkedIn also

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.480
makes money off of showing ads.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.740
And let me just say, it’s extremely expensive
to market on LinkedIn.

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.639
In fact, the average cost of a LinkedIn ad
is $5.26 per click.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:55.599
For perspective, Google Ads on average only
costs $1 to $2 per click.

00:07:55.599 --> 00:08:00.880
So, LinkedIn ads are often 3-5 times more
lucrative than Google Ads.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.650
But with that being said though, people don’t
spend nearly as much time on LinkedIn and

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.889
there’s not as many people are who interested
in buying these ads, so ads only account for

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:12.250
18% of their revenue.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:16.660
And this brings us into LinkedIn’s last
major source of revenue which are premium

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:17.660
subscriptions.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:21.880
For $30 to $60 per month, LinkedIn users can
unlock additional functionality regarding

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:24.129
job postings and career insights.

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:29.870
To be honest, premium isn’t all that useful
for most LinkedIn users but this has nonetheless

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:33.959
grown to account for 17% of LinkedIn’s revenue
over the years.

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:38.399
Going through all of these sources of revenue,
you probably noticed that one attribute was

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:43.750
extremely apparent, the intent of LinkedIn
is extremely focused.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:49.399
You go there to advertise about jobs, hire
for jobs, look for jobs, talk about jobs,

00:08:49.399 --> 00:08:52.209
spy on other people’s jobs, it’s all about
jobs.

00:08:52.209 --> 00:08:58.329
And this is the second factor that makes LinkedIn
more powerful than any other social media:

00:08:58.329 --> 00:09:02.140
the intent and focus of LinkedIn is extremely
clear.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:06.450
With other social media platforms, people
use them for all sorts of reasons from learning

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:09.519
and personal growth to entertainment and comedy.

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:14.740
With LinkedIn, however, there’s only one
reason you use it: something job related.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.519
Other than a few special people, no one actually
uses LinkedIn for fun.

00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:25.010
They use LinkedIn with intent and focus whether
its a company, recruiter, or employee.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:29.900
And given that jobs are the primary way that
the vast majority of the world makes all of

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:34.140
their money, there’s quite a bit money being
exchanged on LinkedIn.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:39.490
And as such, maintaining and facilitating
this network has also proved to be extremely

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:40.750
lucrative.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:45.370
LinkedIn first became profitable all the way
back in March of 2006.

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:50.769
For perspective, it took YouTube at least
a decade to achieve profitability and Twitter,

00:09:50.769 --> 00:09:54.220
Snapchat, WhatsApp, and TikTok are still not
profitable.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:59.800
So, LinkedIn is very much the leader when
it comes to revenue and value per user but

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.000
not everything about LinkedIn is quite as
rosy.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:11.100
TOXIC CRINGE:
By 2011, LinkedIn had grown to over a hundred

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:14.310
million users and they were pulling in more
revenue than Twitter.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:19.680
So, they would decide to make things official
and go public with a valuation of $4.3 billion

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:20.950
in May of 2011.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:26.130
Let’s just say that this IPO was extremely
successful as the stock market would value

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:32.079
the company at over double that at $8.79 billion
just a few weeks later.

00:10:32.079 --> 00:10:38.420
LinkedIn was officially a mainstream platform
but they also started to develop a cringey

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:39.420
reputation.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:44.690
You see, LinkedIn became the place where people
would overstate their experience and inflate

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:48.690
their job titles in hopes of catching the
eyes of recruiters.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:53.420
And I’ll be the first to admit that there
is definitely a cringe factor associated with

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:58.589
LinkedIn, but you can say the same thing about
any social media.

00:10:58.589 --> 00:11:02.579
With Instagram, for example, you’ve got
a bunch of people chasing clout and portraying

00:11:02.579 --> 00:11:06.610
their lives as something better than it is
by posting half naked pictures and highlights

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:08.459
of their otherwise boring lives.

00:11:08.459 --> 00:11:13.089
Similarly, with TikTok, you’ve got a bunch
of thirst traps and mindless content that

00:11:13.089 --> 00:11:15.800
you wont even remember 10 minutes later.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.360
And even on YouTube, you’ve got a bunch
of clickbait and flexing.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:24.680
But, there’s one crucial difference between
the cringe of most social medias and the cringe

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:26.019
of LinkedIn.

00:11:26.019 --> 00:11:31.209
With most social media, the cringe factor
is a result of people chasing something superficial

00:11:31.209 --> 00:11:35.209
whether its the appearance of being rich and
happy or the ego associated with having a

00:11:35.209 --> 00:11:36.680
high follower count.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:41.470
With LinkedIn on the other hand, the cringe
factor is mostly just a result of people wanting

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:44.490
to improve their careers as much as possible.

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:48.649
The bottomline is that people have realized
that staying loyal to one company and working

00:11:48.649 --> 00:11:53.529
for 40 years simply doesn’t play out all
that well over the long term.

00:11:53.529 --> 00:11:59.240
Even if the company treats you well, it’s
more than likely that your peers who constantly

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:03.589
switched jobs and played the game end up in
a better situation.

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:07.940
More and more people are realizing this with
each passing year leading to more and more

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.740
people joining the game on LinkedIn.

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.389
Trying to network with as many people as possible,
getting referrals, contacting recruiters,

00:12:15.389 --> 00:12:17.350
buffing up profiles and so on.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:22.130
This of course leads to some pretty cringey
posts and sterile conversations across the

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:26.240
platform, but hey, it’s all done with mostly
good intentions.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:30.779
Microsoft starting sensing this trend pretty
early on and they basically decided, if you

00:12:30.779 --> 00:12:35.339
can’t fight this movement, you might as
well join this movement and profit off it,

00:12:35.339 --> 00:12:37.370
and that’s exactly what they did.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:43.270
In mid 2016, they went ahead and bought out
Linkedin for a $26.2 billion all cash deal.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:47.110
At the time, Linkedin was pulling in just
under $3 billion per year.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.040
Today, they’re pulling in just under $15
billion per year.

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:55.081
This of course doesn’t directly translate
to market cap growth but I think it’s safe

00:12:55.081 --> 00:12:58.480
to say that Linkedin is worth around a $100
billion today.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:02.980
And this is not surprising when you consider
that they have a billion people using their

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:09.470
platform with the sole focus of career progression,
professional networking, and making more money.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:15.010
And somehow, this insanely powerful platform
is now also owned by Microsoft.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:20.920
It’s becoming harder and harder everyday
to escape Microsoft’s stronghold, so maybe

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.079
it’s time we adopt their strategy and instead
of fighting them, we join them by investing

00:13:25.079 --> 00:13:26.130
in bonds.

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:34.000
If that sounds valuable, check out Silo in
the description below.

